WEBVTT

00:00.210 --> 00:03.750
Up to this point through lower level math and pre-algebra.

00:03.750 --> 00:10.020
We've really just been working with regular numbers like three or zero or negative seven plus maybe

00:10.020 --> 00:11.770
some fractions in decimals.

00:11.790 --> 00:16.050
Now, in algebra, we're really going to need to start working with variables.

00:16.050 --> 00:19.530
And a variable is a symbol for a number that we don't know yet.

00:19.540 --> 00:25.820
So for instance, we most commonly use X or maybe Y to indicate a variable.

00:25.830 --> 00:30.300
So if before algebra we were using almost exclusively constants.

00:30.300 --> 00:31.920
So think about something like this.

00:31.920 --> 00:38.190
Three plus two equals five in this equation, three, two and five are all constants.

00:38.190 --> 00:39.540
They're regular numbers.

00:39.720 --> 00:48.390
If instead of this we replace the three in this equation with the variable x, we can say x plus two

00:48.780 --> 00:49.860
equals five.

00:49.890 --> 00:53.880
It's the same thing, except that now we have a variable in our equation.

00:53.880 --> 00:59.370
And just by matching these up and because we know that we swapped X out for three, we know that the

00:59.370 --> 01:01.790
value of x has to be three.

01:01.800 --> 01:04.770
That's the value that makes this equation two, right?

01:04.770 --> 01:06.960
What do we have to add to two to get five?

01:06.960 --> 01:08.910
Well, that value is three.

01:08.940 --> 01:14.880
We'll talk much, much more later on about how to solve equations like this one and more complicated

01:14.880 --> 01:15.570
equations.

01:15.570 --> 01:22.680
But the idea for now is just that we're going to be using variables like X and Y going forward as placeholders

01:22.680 --> 01:24.540
for values that we don't know.

01:24.540 --> 01:30.150
So in the future we might be presented with something like this just X plus two equals five and ask

01:30.150 --> 01:32.280
to find the value of x.

01:32.280 --> 01:38.100
So at this point, all we want to do with this lesson is define some key vocabulary terms.

01:38.100 --> 01:40.980
So we've already talked about this idea of variables.

01:40.980 --> 01:47.910
We usually use X or Y to represent a variable, but technically we can use any letter that we want.

01:47.910 --> 01:49.710
These are just the most common.

01:49.710 --> 01:55.740
We also want to say that constants are the kinds of real numbers that we're used to seeing like three

01:55.740 --> 02:00.870
or negative seven or in this equation over here, three or two or five are all constants.

02:00.870 --> 02:06.180
The next thing we want to define is the idea of an equation like this one that we wrote here.

02:06.180 --> 02:07.680
X plus two equals five.

02:07.680 --> 02:12.990
And equation is just this mathematical statement here that contains an equal sign.

02:12.990 --> 02:18.300
And what it tells us is that the value on the left side of the equal sign is equal to or equivalent

02:18.300 --> 02:21.210
to the value on the right side of the equal sign.

02:21.210 --> 02:27.810
So like we already looked at in this example, if this left side has to be equal to this right side

02:27.810 --> 02:33.810
because we've set up this equation, then we can start to solve for the value of the variable x, because

02:33.810 --> 02:35.790
we know these two things have to be equal.

02:35.790 --> 02:40.890
And so what we're trying to figure out is how we make this left hand side equal to five and the only

02:40.890 --> 02:44.940
value of x that makes this left hand side equal to five is x equals three.

02:44.940 --> 02:46.980
And so we can solve for the value of that variable.

02:46.980 --> 02:50.760
But we define this kind of mathematical expression as an equation.

02:50.760 --> 02:55.740
If it has an equal sign, it's an equation that implies that the left and right hand sides are equivalent

02:55.740 --> 02:56.760
or equal to each other.

02:56.760 --> 02:57.990
They have an equal value.

02:58.020 --> 03:02.440
We also want to define at this point the idea of a coefficient.

03:02.460 --> 03:05.160
So we talked about variables as x and Y.

03:05.160 --> 03:09.270
Here we looked at the variable in the equation, x plus two equals five.

03:09.270 --> 03:16.530
Well, we can have a coefficient in front of the variable like we're showing here three x, so maybe

03:16.530 --> 03:21.540
we write a different equation, let's call it three x minus ten equals eight.

03:21.540 --> 03:27.840
So in this equation, three is a coefficient on the x variable, and when we have a coefficient on the

03:27.840 --> 03:32.070
variable, it's implied that the coefficient and the variable are multiplied together.

03:32.070 --> 03:36.000
So this three x means three times x or three multiplied by x.

03:36.000 --> 03:41.400
We'll also sometimes see coefficients that aren't real numbers like this three is.

03:41.400 --> 03:43.890
So instead we might see something like this.

03:43.890 --> 03:49.320
Let's say a x squared plus b x plus C.

03:49.320 --> 03:53.160
We know that the variable in this expression is the x variable.

03:53.160 --> 04:01.380
So we have x here and x squared x is the variable, but A and B here are coefficients on the x squared

04:01.380 --> 04:02.850
and X variables.

04:02.850 --> 04:09.930
So A and B represent constant coefficients because A is kind of acting like the three is acting up.

04:09.930 --> 04:13.410
Here it's a coefficient on x squared, it multiplies x squared.

04:13.410 --> 04:18.840
So this is a times x squared and a is just holding the place for some constant.

04:18.840 --> 04:21.210
So is B, it is a constant coefficient.

04:21.210 --> 04:27.870
On x, we usually use letters at the beginning of the alphabet like A and B to represent constants or

04:27.870 --> 04:33.570
constant coefficients, in contrast with usually using letters near the end of the alphabet to represent

04:33.570 --> 04:34.380
variables.

04:34.380 --> 04:37.500
So we have variables, constants, equations, coefficients.

04:37.500 --> 04:43.440
We also have here expressions and we can kind of think about expressions in contrast with equations.

04:43.470 --> 04:47.250
Equations always contain an equal sign, expressions don't.

04:47.250 --> 04:51.390
So an expression is really just a group of terms.

04:51.390 --> 04:55.710
In this expression we have three x as a term and we have ten as a term.

04:55.710 --> 04:59.760
The negative sign, the subtraction is an operator between the two.

04:59.820 --> 05:00.480
Terms.

05:00.480 --> 05:03.840
So we have this three x term minus this ten term.

05:03.840 --> 05:09.450
Putting them together creates an expression, and expression is a very broad term.

05:09.450 --> 05:13.650
We usually use it to identify just any kind of mathematical expression.

05:13.650 --> 05:16.830
That's not explicitly an equation with an equal sign.

05:16.830 --> 05:20.820
So even within this equation we could say x plus two is an expression.

05:20.820 --> 05:26.460
Usually we use expression to indicate more than one term, but we can still use it to indicate a single

05:26.460 --> 05:26.730
term.

05:26.730 --> 05:29.760
So we could even say that five is a mathematical expression.

05:29.760 --> 05:31.530
X plus two is an expression.

05:31.530 --> 05:33.300
Three x minus ten is an expression.

05:33.300 --> 05:37.200
We could say that a x squared plus b x plus C is an expression.

05:37.200 --> 05:38.340
There's no equal sign here.

05:38.340 --> 05:39.450
This is not an equation.

05:39.480 --> 05:41.820
B, X plus C is also an expression.

05:41.850 --> 05:43.680
A x squared plus B x is an expression.

05:43.680 --> 05:46.230
So expression is this very general term.

05:46.230 --> 05:50.670
It's just what we use whenever we don't explicitly have an equation with an equal sign.

05:50.670 --> 05:55.560
And we've also now introduced this other language, this other definition, which is the definition

05:55.560 --> 06:02.190
of a term, and a term is just any single number or variable or numbers and variables multiplied together.

06:02.190 --> 06:09.270
So in this expression here, a x squared is one term, B x is a term, C is a term in this equation,

06:09.270 --> 06:12.780
three x is a term ten is a term eight is a term.

06:12.780 --> 06:14.010
Over here we have examples.

06:14.010 --> 06:16.770
Three x squared could be a term, be x could be a term negative.

06:16.770 --> 06:23.850
One is a term of either a number or a variable or a number and variable multiplied together or a constant

06:23.850 --> 06:25.200
and variable multiplied together.

06:25.200 --> 06:26.550
That is a term.

06:26.550 --> 06:33.120
So just as an example, if we were to consider this equation, three x minus ten equals eight and walk

06:33.120 --> 06:39.150
through all of these new definitions that we have, what can we say about this equation given that we

06:39.150 --> 06:41.310
now have all this new vocabulary?

06:41.310 --> 06:45.810
Well, we could say that in this equation we have an X variable.

06:45.810 --> 06:48.210
We know that that X right there is a variable.

06:48.210 --> 06:50.730
We could say that this whole thing is an equation.

06:50.730 --> 06:55.980
So three x minus ten equals eight is the equation.

06:55.980 --> 07:02.550
In contrast with that, we could say that three x minus ten is an expression.

07:02.550 --> 07:07.260
We could even list any one of these terms individually as an expression.

07:07.260 --> 07:10.830
But again, typically an expression is a collection of more than one term.

07:10.830 --> 07:17.070
So calling three x minus ten and expression from this equation is probably the best use of expression

07:17.070 --> 07:18.840
when we're looking at this equation.

07:18.840 --> 07:25.020
But we can also identify the individual terms three, x, ten and eight.

07:25.020 --> 07:27.450
Those are all terms in this equation.

07:27.450 --> 07:34.290
We could recognize that the coefficient is three, the coefficient on the variable is three, and that

07:34.290 --> 07:42.690
we have constants here of ten and eight, because individually here we have ten and eight in the equation.

07:42.690 --> 07:48.960
So all we want to take away here is this idea that we're going to start replacing real numbers with

07:48.960 --> 07:52.680
letters instead, and that we call those letters variables.

07:52.680 --> 07:58.650
Variables are attached to this idea of constants because we distinguish between a variable and a constant.

07:58.650 --> 08:04.110
We see variables appearing in equations and expressions, and we now know the difference between an

08:04.110 --> 08:05.520
equation and an expression.

08:05.520 --> 08:12.120
We see coefficients attached to variables, coefficients that multiply variables, and we know that

08:12.120 --> 08:18.330
variables can be part of individual terms, but that terms don't necessarily have to contain a variable.

08:18.420 --> 08:24.210
A term can be just a variable, just a constant or a constant multiplied by a variable.
